WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our next guest is an Emmy and
Tony Award-winning actor

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.205
you know from shows such as "The
Practice," "Boston Legal,"

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:07.407
"The Librarians" and
"The John Larroquette Show."

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:10.577
He returns as Dan Fielding in
the revival of "Night Court,"

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:12.846
which premieres with
back-to-back episodes

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.781
on January 17th here on NBC.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:16.082
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:17.584
-Alright, look. I just want to
say this one thing.

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:21.053
I'm only here to help out
temporarily, a few days.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:22.655
-I'm hearing weeks.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:23.923
-If you keep smiling like that,

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:25.992
I'm out of here right now,
so just knock it off.

00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:27.693
That's better.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:29.762
-"Better, Your Honor."

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:31.931
-[ Chuckles ]
I'm already regretting this.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.633
-Please welcome to the show
the one, the only

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:34.901
John Larroquette, everybody.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.569
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:44.210 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:51.851 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:54.720
How are you?
-I'm still alive.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.356
-You're still alive.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:59.092
We're proving it.
We're proving it on live TV.

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.160
It's so exciting.
-Oh, this is so great.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.329
I've not been in front of a live
audience in quite a while.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.664
-It's really nice, isn't it?

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.099
-It's invigorating.
It's really good to hear.

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:09.235
[ Cheers and applause ]
-It makes a huge difference.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.370
This is very exciting for me.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.439
This is a show that I remember

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.074
being a huge fan of back
in the day.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:16.408
It ran for nine seasons.

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:18.377
These are sort of things that
don't happen anymore.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:19.545
I think everybody knew it.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:22.348
It was nominated for
over 30 Emmys.

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.484
Is it a trip to return to it
30 years later?

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.320
-Oh, an absolute trip, yes.
I mean, it was a trip that

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.091
I reluctantly, finally decided
to join after a lot of cajoling,

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:34.893
from Melissa Rauch particularly.

00:01:34.893 --> 00:01:37.029
-Who is the lovely judge
in that scene. Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:38.431
-She is a lovely judge.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.800
And because of the way it was
presented to me, you know,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.336
as an actor, I became aware
that, "Oh, wait,

00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:46.672
hold on a second.
How often does one get a chance

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:49.308
to look at a character that you
played 30 years ago

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:52.344
and see him now
30 years in the future?

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:53.713
What's happened to him?"

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:56.715
As an actor, it seemed like
an interesting problem

00:01:56.715 --> 00:01:59.018
to actually solve a little bit.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.120
-And it has.
There is a huge change, right?

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.189
Like, Dan Fielding was
arrogance incarnate.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.025
Like, that was my memory of him,
a prosecutor.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:08.561
And now you've obviously been
changed by life.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.563
Dan has, I should say.
Enter a public defender.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:12.532
That must be one of the fun
parts is how it's different.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.234
-Yeah, it's very different.
It takes him a while to realize

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:16.502
that's what he's supposed
to be doing

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:18.771
because a few times the judge
has to remind him,

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:21.774
as he's scolding the defendant
and telling the judge

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.711
what a piece of crap
this guy is, come on.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.813
She goes, "You know, you're
supposed to be defending him."

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:27.881
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.282
"Oh. Well, no.
You didn't let me finish.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.717
This young man is such
a pillar of society.

00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:34.420
How could you possibly think
he did anything wrong?"

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.289
So, it was an interesting
dilemma.

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:41.160
And also, the idea that this
woman is the daughter of

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.129
Harry Stone, you know, made --
and that's what really melts

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:46.466
Dan Fielding to
some degree of humanity

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.601
to want to go and help her.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.769
-It is a lot of similarities.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:52.372
The set certainly brings me
back.

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:55.841
The theme song is a little
different and was tweaked by --

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:59.912
from within your family.
-Yes, my youngest son was born

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:03.015
during the heyday of
"Night Court" in 1987.

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:05.151
And Benjamin is an excellent
musician,

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.019
graduate of the Berklee College
of Music.

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:10.089
And my middle son is also
an artist and musician.

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.258
My daughter --
very artistic family.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.793
But Ben called me one day
and said "Listen.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.828
Listen to what I did.
I just did it."

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:18.630
He did it from memory and just
recorded it himself.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.967
And so I thought, "That's a nice
new take on that."

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:23.670
So I showed it to Melissa
and Dan Rubin

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.606
with no intention of going,
"Hey, hey. Nepo, nepo, nepo."

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.107
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:29.575
-Yeah, you should never say
"nepo" out loud.

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:30.609
That's one of the things
we heard.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:32.111
[ Laughter ]
-There you are. Yeah.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:34.046
And they went, "Oh, my God.
This is fantastic.

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:36.616
It's perfect." And so he wound
up being the composer.

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:38.851
And he does all the interstitial
music within the --

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.519
So he is the composer of
the new "Night Court."

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:42.889
-That's really nice.
I want to ask,

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.491
having played a lawyer more than
once on television,

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.928
do you think you would make
a good lawyer?

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.796
-Um, I don't know.

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:52.965
I certainly wouldn't be a very
good public defender because I

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:56.969
would be unable to not take
money from people.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.671
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:00.606
It's very noble that you know
that about yourself.

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:02.041
[ Laughter ]
-Yes, listen.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:03.809
One accepts one's shortcomings

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:05.677
and tries to make
the best of them,

00:04:05.677 --> 00:04:07.513
as we all do in life.

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.049
I don't know, and I don't know
why -- You know, I'm 6'5."

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:13.586
I have a deep voice.
Perhaps that seems somewhat

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.788
judicial or something,
you know.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.890
And I played lawyers before
"Night Court."

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.827
You know, my very early career,
I was a lawyer on "Dallas"

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:24.430
of all things, a series that was
around in the late '70s.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.231
So it wasn't just "Night Court"
that started that.

00:04:26.231 --> 00:04:28.801
But certainly, there was
a cavalcade of them

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.603
after "Night Court" came along.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.739
Serious and comedic as well.

00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:36.142
-You were in a show
that I feel -- I remember

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:37.776
feeling very special
at the time.

00:04:37.776 --> 00:04:39.512
It was critically adored.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:42.281
It never quite became the sort
of ratings monster

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:44.917
that "Night Court" was, which
was "The John Larroquette Show."

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:46.452
Was that a show you look back on

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:49.221
and reflect on fondly
for how different it was?

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:50.722
-Yes, definitely fondly.

00:04:50.722 --> 00:04:52.492
The only thing I don't like,
I hated the title.

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:54.193
I liked the show.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:55.928
Now, I should note
"The John Larroquette Show,"

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.830
not your idea.
-Not my idea at all.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.132
I begged Warren Littlefield,
who was president of the network

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:01.567
at the time,
not to name it that

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.136
because -- and it was completely
ego because I knew eventually

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:05.571
one day I was going to look at
the Nielsen ratings

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:08.841
and it was gonna go, "number 76,
'The John Larroquette Show.'"

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.309
[ Laughter ]

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:13.980
But because of "Night Court,"
because of the first season,

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.481
we were pitted against
"Roseanne"

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.882
when she was in her heyday.
-Yeah.

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:19.685
-They thought at least that will
bring some people into the tent.

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:21.387
And I lost that battle.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.990
But I think the idea of the
show, Don Reo's script that

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.226
I found, the first season of
that show I thought was --

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:28.727
I don't say this modestly --

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:30.963
some of the best sitcom
television ever made.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.833
And the show changed because
wanting to try and get numbers,

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.268
wanting to try and get a larger
audience in.

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:38.638
It sort of changed,
and it was still funny.

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:40.506
Mitch Hurwitz came along
a couple years later,

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:42.975
ran it before he did
"Arrested Development,"

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:44.343
great comedy writer.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:45.845
But it just sort of
lost its soul

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.213
a little bit and lost its way.

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:49.215
But, you know,
almost four years.

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.583
You can't really call it a flop
being on the air

00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:53.252
for almost 100 episodes.
-No.

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:55.755
And again, as we talk about the
way the landscape has changed,

00:05:55.755 --> 00:05:57.289
100 episodes now feels
like a million.

00:05:57.289 --> 00:05:59.258
So it's really cool to be able
to do that much

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:02.227
storytelling with one show.
-I also do think that had we

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:04.897
done that show 10 years in the
future, when there was

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:07.633
a little more freedom in
the cable world of being able

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.769
to deal with certain subjects
that that show

00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:11.470
would have had longer legs.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.205
-I'm sure you still get
recognized from that.

00:06:13.205 --> 00:06:14.640
Obviously, you get recognized
from "Night Court."

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.942
Is there any work you did way
back before those that you still

00:06:16.942 --> 00:06:19.078
get recognized for that is
surprising to you?

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:21.080
-Yeah, "The Texas Chain Saw
Massacre."

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.349
-So, now you are -- What did you

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.117
do in "The Texas Chain Saw
Massacre"?

00:06:25.117 --> 00:06:27.053
-The opening narration,
that's it.

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:28.988
-You were the opening narration?

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:30.656
How did that come to be?

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:33.959
-In the mid '60s somewhere --
I forget --

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:36.028
like Robin said, "if you
remember the '60s,

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:38.431
you weren't there."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:41.566
Living in a little town in
Colorado, being a bartender,

00:06:41.566 --> 00:06:44.003
a fellow came in, we started
talking, a fellow named

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:45.471
Tobe Hooper and he said,

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:46.638
"Hey, I'm going to be
a movie director."

00:06:46.638 --> 00:06:48.307
I went, "Yeah, sure.
Have another Coors."

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:49.708
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:50.942
And he eventually went to L.A.

00:06:50.942 --> 00:06:52.178
I eventually went to L.A.

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:53.845
Somehow, he found out I was
in L.A.

00:06:53.845 --> 00:06:56.449
He called and said, "I made
this movie for nothing,

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:59.418
but it's a little horror film.
I need a favor."

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.620
And so I did this opening
narration of it.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:02.889
I've never seen the film.

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:04.624
[ Laughter ]

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:07.159
Nor have I seen any of
the subsequent films

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:08.727
of "Texas Chain Saw Massacre."

00:07:08.727 --> 00:07:11.197
-You're, like, waiting to do,
like, one big binge?

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:13.231
-Yes. Yes, right.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.231 --> 00:07:16.102
And I actually did the
subsequent ones as well

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:18.471
and actually got money for them
as the years went on.

00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:20.038
I think I've done three of them.

00:07:20.038 --> 00:07:22.275
But still have never seen them,
not without Tobe.

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.509
Other people have done them.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.845
But it's amazing to me that it
has stuck,

00:07:25.845 --> 00:07:28.848
that it -- that --
Horror is not really my genre.

00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:30.916
So I haven't been to
the midnight shows

00:07:30.916 --> 00:07:34.119
of it or anything, but a lot of
people love that movie

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:35.621
and remember the fact that --

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:37.156
And I don't even think I got
a credit on it.

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:38.857
I don't think my name is
anywhere on it.

00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:41.460
But people seem to have known
that I was the narrator.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.396
-Well, that is a very cool first
way to break into the business.

00:07:44.396 --> 00:07:46.999
And it is so nice you've stayed
in the business.

00:07:46.999 --> 00:07:48.033
And it's so great
to have you here.

00:07:48.033 --> 00:07:50.135
Thank you so much.
John Larroquette.

00:07:50.135 --> 00:07:52.872
"Night Court" premieres with
back-to-back episodes

00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:55.641
on January 17th and then will
air Tuesday nights

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:56.876
at 8:00 p.m. on NBC.

00:07:56.876 --> 00:07:58.578
We'll be right back with
more "Late Night."

